  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry this afternoon, though a rogue shower or \nthunderstorm remains possible through sunset.\n\nSeasonably warm and muggy this afternoon, though many locations will \nremain dry. Earlier cloud cover resulted in lower CAPE values and, \nin some instances, a weak cap along and east of I-95. However, that \ncap is eroding, with isolated showers and garden-variety \nthunderstorms developing. With the approach of a cold front this \nafternoon, a rogue pop-up shower or thunderstorm remains possible; \nhowever, coverage should be sparse.\n\nStorms should remain sub-severe, though sufficient DCAPE of 800-1000 \nJ/kg exists mainly west of I-495 in MA and across northern and \ncentral CT. Any storms that develop in these areas could produce \ngusty straight-line winds. The best timing for storms is between 2 \nand 8 PM. With the loss of daytime heating, any storms that develop \nwill quickly diminish. That being said, there is nothing worth \ncanceling outdoor plans over.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A fall-like feel with seasonable temperatures and \nvery low humidity Saturday, becoming more summer-like Sunday. The \nweekend remains dry, with increasing clouds late Sunday.\n\nA post-cold-frontal air mass arrives overnight, ushering in \nrefreshing conditions and setting the stage for a nice weekend. High \npressure moves in from the northwest, leading to dry weather both \nSaturday and Sunday.\n\nDew points Saturday will be fall-like, mainly in the 50s. However, \nthe lower humidity will be short-lived, as southwest flow returns \nSunday and brings dew points back into the middle and upper 60s. \nOtherwise, temperatures will be seasonable Saturday, with highs in \nthe upper 70s to around 80 degrees, then slightly warmer Sunday, \nwith highs in the lower to middle 80s.\n\nAs mentioned, Sunday remains dry, though clouds increase during the \nafternoon ahead of the next chance for rain. A few cool nights are \nalso expected, allowing for a break from the air conditioning. \nTemperatures fall into the 50s overnight into Saturday morning and \nagain Saturday night into Sunday morning, though urban centers \nremain in the lower 60s.\n\nAdditionally, there is a moderate risk for rip currents Saturday. \nThose heading to the beach should check with beach staff or \nlifeguards regarding rip current conditions before entering the \nwater. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Cloudy and unsettled early to mid next week \nwith several opportunities for showers and storms. While Monday \nnow looks cloudy but generally dry, better chances for showers \nand storms are anticipated for Tue through Thurs.\n\nStill looks unsettled during the early to middle part of the \nworkweek. A backdoor cool front looks to build westward through \nSouthern New England on Mon with generally dry but cloudy weather, \nthen remain to our southwest Tue and Wed. Periods of showers and \nstorms now seem more favored around Tue and Wed versus Mon. Thurs \nmay offer a better shot at widespread showers and storms regionally, \nas a cold front looks to progress the stalled frontal boundary \nsouthward. Expect a narrower range in high/low temps Mon through \nWed, with highs running a little cooler than normal and lows \nslightly milder than normal.",
